    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="truth">The truth about "passive"</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Passive income is money that keeps coming in after the work is done, not money that comes in with no work at all. The effort is real — it's just paid up front, once, instead of every time you get paid.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Compare it to a job, where you trade an hour for a wage and the trade resets tomorrow. A passive asset breaks that link: you build the thing once, and it earns on its own schedule afterward. <strong>The word "passive" describes the income, never the building.</strong> Confuse the two and you'll quit during the only part that counts — the front-loaded build.</p>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>A cleaner word than "passive" is "leveraged." You're not avoiding work — you're making one piece of work pay more than once. Every passive asset is just effort that refuses to be spent only a single time.</p></div>

