    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="front">What you front-load, and in what order</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">You front-load three things, in order: the asset that earns, the place it's sold, and the traffic that finds it. Build them in that sequence — asset first — and each step has something real to hang on.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The order matters because each piece depends on the one before it. Traffic to nothing is wasted. A checkout with no product is empty. <strong>Build the asset first, give it a home second, point traffic at it third</strong> — and resist the urge to do all three at once and finish none.</p>

    <div class="framework reveal">
      <span class="tag"><span>✦</span> The Front-Load Order</span>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="node"><div class="h">The Asset</div><div class="t">The product or content that earns — built once.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">The Home</div><div class="t">A checkout or page where the sale happens.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">The Traffic</div><div class="t">Evergreen search that keeps finding it.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Chasing traffic before the asset exists. Growing an audience with nothing to sell them is building the queue before the shop. Build the thing worth arriving for first — then send people to it.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="stack">When one stream becomes a system</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">One stream becomes a system when you build related assets for the same buyer — a second product, an affiliate recommendation, a bundle — so each new piece compounds on the audience and trust the first one earned.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This is where passive income stops being one lucky product and starts being a business. The first asset proved the buyer exists; every asset after it is cheaper to build and easier to sell, because you're no longer starting from zero. <strong>Streams don't add — they compound, each one making the next one land harder.</strong></p>

    <div class="notebook reveal">
