      <p>learn who actually bought it →</p>
      <p>build the next thing that buyer needs →</p>
      <p>point the same traffic at both →</p>
      <p>and every stream makes the last one worth more.</p>
      <span class="pin-note">one becomes a system ♡</span>
    </div>
    <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/affiliate-marketing-101">Affiliate Marketing 101 →</a></p>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="traffic">The part that stays manual (for a while)</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">The traffic engine is the part that stays hands-on longest — you keep making pins and content to feed it until it's built enough momentum to run mostly on its own. The asset is passive; feeding it isn't, at first.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Be honest with yourself about this part. The product earns passively, but early on you're still the one creating the content that sends people to it. That's the front-loaded work in its ongoing form — and it fades as your evergreen content accumulates and starts doing the finding for you. <strong>The goal isn't zero work on day one; it's less work every month.</strong></p>

    <div class="box action reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">→</span> Action Step</span><h4>Build one asset, then feed it</h4><p>Make one thing that earns on a link. Then spend your working time pointing evergreen content at it — not building a second asset yet. One stream, fed consistently, teaches you the whole model.</p><a class="btn dark" href="/learn/automating-your-online-business">The Automation Guide <span class="a">→</span></a></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">07</span><div class="num">Chapter Seven</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="patience">Why passive income needs patience</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Passive income needs patience because the earning comes after the building, and the gap between them is where most people quit. The asset you build this month often earns most a year from now, as traffic compounds.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This is the part the fantasy erased. There's a lag — sometimes a long one — between front-loading the work and seeing it pay. The people who succeed aren't more talented; they're the ones who kept feeding the asset through the quiet stretch before it caught. <strong>The lag is the price. Patience is how you pay it.</strong></p>

